{"name":"Vers de Terre","hex":"#acdfad","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vers","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Clipping of versatile."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Abbreviation of versine or versed sine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vers"}],"entries":[{"title":"Earthworm","blocks":[{"text":"An earthworm is a soil-dwelling terrestrial invertebrate that belongs to the phylum Annelida. The term is the common name for the largest members of the class Oligochaeta. In classical systems, they were in the order of Opisthopora since the male pores opened posterior to the female pores, although the internal male segments are anterior to the female. Theoretical cladistic studies have placed them in the suborder Lumbricina of the order Haplotaxida, but this may change. Other slang names for earthworms include \"dew-worm\", \"rainworm\", \"nightcrawler\", and \"angleworm\". Larger terrestrial earthworms are also called megadriles as opposed to the microdriles in the families Tubificidae (semiaquatic), Lumbricidae and Enchytraeidae (terrestrial). The megadriles are characterized by a distinct clitellum and a vascular system with true capillaries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Eisenia_foetida_R.H._%288%29.JPG/330px-Eisenia_foetida_R.H._%288%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earthworm","wikidata":"Q124378"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Eisenia_foetida_R.H._%288%29.JPG/500px-Eisenia_foetida_R.H._%288%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Eisenia_foetida_R.H._%288%29.JPG/1280px-Eisenia_foetida_R.H._%288%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Eisenia_foetida_R.H._%288%29.JPG/500px-Eisenia_foetida_R.H._%288%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEisenia_foetida_R.H._(8).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Earthworm_nervous_system.png/500px-Earthworm_nervous_system.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Earthworm_nervous_system.png/500px-Earthworm_nervous_system.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nervous system of the anterior end of an earthworm","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEarthworm_nervous_system.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Earthworm_setae.jpg/500px-Earthworm_setae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Earthworm_setae.jpg/1280px-Earthworm_setae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Earthworm_setae.jpg/500px-Earthworm_setae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A profile SEM image of Lumbricus terrestris setae, small bristle-like projections that facilitate movement by anchoring the earthworm in the soil.  ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEarthworm_setae.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Mating_earthworms.jpg/500px-Mating_earthworms.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Mating_earthworms.jpg/500px-Mating_earthworms.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Earthworm copulation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMating_earthworms